One of the biggest objections to GitOps is:

“We can’t put secrets in Git.”
Fair enough.
But the solution isn’t to abandon GitOps — it’s to do secrets the right way.

Here’s what we use (and recommend to clients) when managing secrets declaratively in production environments 👇

🔑 3 Battle-Tested Strategies for Secrets in GitOps
1️⃣ Sealed Secrets (Bitnami)
🔒 Public-key encryption
✅ Safe to commit to Git
✅ Works great with ArgoCD sync
📦 Easy to integrate via CRDs

📌 Pro Tip: Rotate the controller’s private key periodically to reduce long-term risk.

2️⃣ SOPS + KMS (GPG, AWS, GCP, Azure)
💡 Encrypt individual values or full YAML
✅ Works with Kustomize
✅ Git-friendly, fine-grained control
✅ Keys stored securely in cloud KMS

📌 Pro Tip: Store .sops.yaml in each env folder to define per-environment encryption logic.

3️⃣ ArgoCD Vault Plugin
🔌 Fetch secrets from Vault, AWS SSM, or others at deploy time
✅ Secrets stay out of Git
✅ Dynamic templating support
✅ No need to commit encrypted files

📌 Pro Tip: Use with helmfile or kustomize workflows for structured secret injection.

💭 What to Avoid
🚫 Plain Secrets.yaml in Git (even base64)
🚫 Mixing secrets with app config
🚫 One-size-fits-all solutions
🚫 Relying on “manual patching” outside Git

📌 TL;DR
GitOps and secrets can work together — if you use the right tools and workflows.
Choose what fits your security model, not just what’s easy.
